>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `BLAST_TAC`

``` hol4
tableauLib.BLAST_TAC : thm list -> tactic
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Solves a goal by classical tableau search and kernel-checked replay.

After built-in preprocessing, `BLAST_TAC thms` tries increasing tableau
resource bounds from 0 through `!tableauLib.depth_limit`, whose initial
value is 20. The reference is read when the tactic is applied. Safe
non-instantiating inferences are free; unsafe and instantiating inferences
consume the bound according to the tableau's branching penalty. There is
no timeout.

The theorem list locally modifies the current classical rule set. The
markers `SIntro`, `Intro`, `SElim`, `Elim`, `SDest`, `Dest` and `Del` add
or remove rules. Every unmarked theorem is inserted as an assumption before
tableau translation, so it becomes an ordinary branch formula.
Content-bearing generic simplifier wrappers are unwrapped and inserted;
inert generic controls are ignored.

The rule nets include both safe directions of datatype constructor
injectivity supplied by `TypeBase`, allowing the tableau to decompose and
rebuild constructor equalities.

The raw `CS_BLAST_DEPTH_TAC` export does not change `BLAST_TAC`'s public
packaging: preprocessing, iterative deepening through `depth_limit`, typed
reconstruction, and tracing retain their existing behaviour.

A candidate tableau is replayed through HOL's typed inference kernel. If
replay fails, search backtracks and tries another candidate. Set the
`"blast"` trace with `Feedback.set_trace`: level 1 reports replay failures
and rejected elimination rules, level 2 also reports search statistics,
and level 3 or more includes full tableau traces.

This tactic has deliberate limitations. Classical safe and unsafe wrappers
are ignored because the tableau consults only rule nets. Weak elimination
rules, whose premises lose the arbitrary conclusion, cannot be represented
and are skipped. Search uses first-order rather than higher-order
unification, so rules requiring higher-order unification may be missed.
Equality handling is simple and incomplete: restricted substitution from
suitable equality assumptions is provided, but not a complete equational
calculus.

This is a general classical prover. It is unrelated to
`blastLib.BBLAST_TAC`, which expands finite-word operations into Boolean
bit formulas.

### Failure

Fails unless preprocessing or a reconstructible tableau proof closes the
goal within the configured bounds. Malformed extra rules can also fail
during marker processing.

### Example

``` hol4
Theorem blast_tac_example:
  (!x. P x ==> Q x) ==> P a ==> Q a
Proof
  tableauLib.BLAST_TAC []
QED
```

